WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.746
Two threads add one to a shared counter.

2
00:00:05.746 --> 00:00:06.660
It holds 41.

3
00:00:06.867 --> 00:00:11.076
Each thread reads the value, adds one, and writes the result back.

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.701
Thread 1 writes 42.

5
00:00:12.701 --> 00:00:14.766
Then thread 2 writes 42 too.

6
00:00:14.766 --> 00:00:18.232
Two increments, and the counter went up by one.

7
00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:20.166
The second write was blind.

8
00:00:20.166 --> 00:00:24.146
It never checked whether the value had changed since its read.

9
00:00:24.146 --> 00:00:25.365
One update is lost.

10
00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:29.026
Compare-and-set makes the write conditional.

11
00:00:29.026 --> 00:00:32.289
You pass the value you expect, and the value you want.

12
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.981
If memory still holds the expected value, it stores the new one and returns true.

13
00:00:37.981 --> 00:00:41.025
If not, it changes nothing and returns false.

14
00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:43.366
It does all of this as one step.

15
00:00:43.366 --> 00:00:47.364
On x86, HotSpot compiles it to one lock cmpxchg instruction.

16
00:00:47.364 --> 00:00:55.227
While it runs, the core keeps the cache line to itself, so no other core can get in between the compare and the store.

17
00:00:56.133 --> 00:00:59.288
Run the race again, with compareAndSet.

18
00:00:59.288 --> 00:01:00.987
Both threads read 41.

19
00:01:00.987 --> 00:01:02.767
Both want to store 42.

20
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.433
Thread 1 goes first.

21
00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:09.307
Memory still holds 41, so the store happens, and thread 1 gets true.

22
00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:13.808
Now thread 2 asks for the same thing, 41 to 42.

23
00:01:13.808 --> 00:01:15.736
But memory holds 42 now.

24
00:01:15.736 --> 00:01:19.269
So nothing changes, and thread 2 gets false.

25
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.200
Its decision was based on old data.

26
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.208
So it throws it away, and starts again.

27
00:01:25.208 --> 00:01:26.133
It reads 42.

28
00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:28.121
It asks for 42 to 43.

29
00:01:28.121 --> 00:01:32.631
This time memory still holds 42, so the store happens.

30
00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:34.982
41, then 42, then 43.

31
00:01:34.982 --> 00:01:37.232
Both increments count.

32
00:01:37.232 --> 00:01:38.869
Nothing is lost.

33
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.621
In code, this is a loop.

34
00:01:41.621 --> 00:01:43.988
Here it is in Java and in Kotlin.

35
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:45.529
Read the value.

36
00:01:45.733 --> 00:01:49.751
Store seen plus one, but only if the counter still holds seen.

37
00:01:49.967 --> 00:01:52.824
If that returns false, someone changed it.

38
00:01:52.824 --> 00:01:54.524
Go round, and read again.

39
00:01:55.233 --> 00:02:01.512
Any read-modify-write fits this shape: read, decide, then store only if nothing changed.

40
00:02:01.733 --> 00:02:04.309
The lab's max tracker is the same loop.

41
00:02:04.309 --> 00:02:11.176
Its decide step is one more line: if the amount is not bigger, there is nothing to store, so it returns.

42
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.913
A failed CAS costs a retry, not a wait.

43
00:02:13.913 --> 00:02:16.555
With little contention, retries are rare.

44
00:02:16.555 --> 00:02:23.900
With many threads on one variable, they keep failing and re-reading, and the cache line bounces from core to core.
